==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒkl̩/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-cockle.wav|Audio (RP)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒkəl|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
[[File:Coque blanche (Cerastoderma edule).jpg|thumb|Cockles]]
From {{inh|en|enm|cokel}}, {{m|enm|cokkel}}, {{m|enm|kokkel}}, {{m|enm|cocle}}, of uncertain origin. Perhaps a diminutive of {{der|en|enm|cokke|id=cockle}}, {{m|enm|cok|id=cockle|t=cockle}}, from {{inh|en|ang|cocc}} (found in {{m|ang|sǣcocc|t=cockle}}) {{suffix|en||le}}; or perhaps from {{der|en|fro|coquille}}, from {{der|en|VL.|*cocchilia}}, from {{m|la|conchylia}}, from {{der|en|grc|κογχύλιον}}, diminutive of {{m|grc|κογχύλη||mussel}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*konkho}}.{{attention|ine-pro|this PIE looks dodgy}}

====Noun====
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# Any of various [[edible]] [[European]] [[bivalve]] [[mollusk]]s, of the [[family]] [[Cardiidae]], having [[heart]]-[[shaped]] [[shell]]s.
#* '''1990''', Dido Davies, Andrew Davies, ''William Gerhardie: A Biography'' (page 164)
#*: His wife, a small woman who walked always on high heels, borrowed Gerhardie's primus stove several times a day to cook her husband gargantuan meals of '''cockles''', mussels, snails, and other such unpalatables.
# The [[shell]] of such a mollusk.
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} One’s [[innermost]] [[feeling]]s (only in the expression “[[warm the cockles of one's heart|the cockles of one’s heart]]”).
# {{lb|en|directly from French coquille}} A [[wrinkle]], [[pucker]]
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A defect in [[sheepskin]]; firm dark [[nodule]]s caused by the bites of [[ked]]s on live sheep
# {{lb|en|mining|UK|Cornwall}} The [[mineral]] black tourmaline or [[schorl]]<ref>{{R:Raymond Glossary}}</ref>.
# {{lb|en|UK}} The fire chamber of a [[furnace]]<ref>{{R:Knight AMD}}</ref>.
# {{lb|en|UK}} A [[kiln]] for drying [[hops]]; an [[oast]]<ref>{{R:Knight AMD}}</ref>.
# {{lb|en|UK}} The dome of a heating furnace<ref>{{R:Knight AMD}}</ref>.

